## Bitly-standard addendum (conformed 2026-08-11)

Adopted verbatim from the canonical bitly cortex so this project's brain discipline matches the most up-to-date standard. Where these overlap rules above, the stricter wording here wins.

- **Reference library:** A shared, on-disk reference library lives at `~/github/knowledge` (see `knowledge.mdc`), organized by domain (e.g. `hack/`). When Victor asks "do you have a reference?" or needs authoritative backing, search it FIRST and answer in the form "yes, in <source> there's a detail that points out that <exact point>", with the file path. NEVER cite a source that is not actually in the library.
- **Confirmation line requires a REAL write, every turn.** At the end of every exchange, persist to the DB FIRST, then show: `Updated BRAIN <data in the db>`. That line is a CLAIM that a `sqlite3` INSERT/UPDATE actually executed THIS turn. NEVER print it unless the write ran this turn and succeeded. Printing it without the write is fabrication and is forbidden: do not defer, do not "print now and save later". If genuinely nothing changed, run no write and say so plainly.
- **NEVER fabricate data.** Work only with real, provable, testable data. If it is not in `brain.db`, not from a verified tool call, and not explicitly provided by Victor, the answer is "I don't know", so ASK. Every factual claim must trace to a real source.
- **Save MAXIMUM detail, never a few bits.** Every brain write must capture full context: specifics, dates, names, keys, numbers, direct quotes, reasoning, and surrounding circumstances. Verbosity and completeness are correct; brevity is the bug. When in doubt, write MORE.
